I’ll inspect the specimen metadata, pattern, and the frozen analysis/tooling guidance first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, build a complete multiphase GSAS-II refinement, validate residual peak families and physical geometry, and export the requested replayable project and files.

The chemistry is a 1:1 MgO/WO₃ reaction fired at 900 °C, so MgWO₄ is the chemically ordinary principal product; residual MgO, WO₃, or an off-stoichiometric Mg–W–O phase remain plausible until the diffraction families are accounted for. I’m now verifying the configured scientific environment and frozen COD index before any phase assignment.

The framework’s evolution-controller check reports only that the separate workshop/controller is not deployed. That controller is explicitly outside this specimen-only task; the local COD index, DICVOL, materials Python stack, and GSAS-II remain available, so I’ll continue with those standalone scientific tools.

The peak-family map already supports three chemically ordinary phases: wolframite-type MgWO₄ dominates; the 23.43/23.93/24.67° triplet and its higher-angle companions identify monoclinic WO₃; and the isolated 43.21° line with 62.54°/75.00° companions identifies residual MgO. I’ll now discriminate among same-composition references and refine the full three-phase state before considering anything less ordinary.

The full three-phase model is converging and gives sensible first-pass abundances near 81 wt% MgWO₄, 15 wt% WO₃, and 5 wt% MgO. The initial simultaneous zero/height fit is highly correlated and briefly wandered beyond the laboratory displacement range, so I’m treating that checkpoint as provisional and will stabilize calibration against refined cells before accepting fractions.

The stabilized calibration now sits inside the stated laboratory ranges (zero ≈ +0.05°, specimen shift ≈ −0.46 mm), and the complete model reaches Rwp ≈ 10.21% before coordinate/specimen refinements. I’m comparing the two physically valid room-temperature monoclinic WO₃ settings on identical terms; their topology is equivalent, so the decision will be based on broad intensity-family agreement and geometry, not a marginal numerical gain.

The isotropic-size branches expose a shallow instrument/size ridge: equally good fits span X≈1–3 with MgWO₄ and WO₃ domains ≈0.16–0.27 µm. I’m retaining the interior, unconstrained solution (X≈1.06; domains ≈0.16 µm) as the physical checkpoint and testing whether phase-specific strain is actually supported; bound-hitting alternatives will be rejected even if their Rwp differs only in the third decimal place.

Phase-specific strain is supported only for WO₃, but its magnitude is strongly correlated with the global Gaussian profile; the unconstrained fit drives U to the top of the documented range. I’ve preserved that as a provisional profile checkpoint and am now isolating polarization and surface roughness with all profile/cell terms held fixed, so their support is judged cleanly rather than through parameter drift.

The bounded specimen test supports strong low-angle attenuation: a physical Suortti roughness model with A fixed at its allowed lower edge (0.05), B≈0.12, and polarization fixed at the allowed lower edge (0.50) reduces Rwp reproducibly to ≈8.08%. These edge values are correlated, so I’ll report that limitation. I’m now refining heavy-atom coordinates phase by phase, with profile and cells held, and will retain each step only if bond geometry remains ordinary.

Both accepted phases show only very weak texture (March–Dollase ratios within about 2% of 1); MgWO₄ [001] gives the only clear, though small, improvement. WO₃ oxygen refinement is statistically underdetermined and moved several sites far beyond the documented reference-mismatch scale despite only ΔRwp≈0.07%, so I rolled that branch back and retained the published oxygen topology. Next I’m testing phase displacement factors and then one final controlled profile release.

The accepted full model is now replay-stable (Rwp 7.72239% before replay and 7.72238% after), with all profile and structural values interior to their physical ranges. Fractions at this checkpoint are about 76.48% MgWO₄, 15.38% WO₃, and 8.14% MgO. I’ll do one last isolated test for phase-specific strain; if it is not interior and replay-stable, this checkpoint becomes final.

The final supported complexity is uniaxial WO₃ microstrain along [010] (≈4479/6008 ppm equatorial/axial), with all values interior and a replay-stable Rwp ≈7.643%. I’m now auditing the final residual against the reflection lists, then I’ll export the three CIFs, full-grid fit profile, normalized fractions/statistics, report, and the exact final GPX.

The three-phase model is complete and all coherent reflection families are assigned to MgWO₄, WO₃, and MgO. I’m checking the final replay-stable refinement now, then I’ll export the CIFs, profile, project, and concise uncertainty report.

Fixing the correlated instrumental X term removed the soft singularity without changing the fit or phase fractions materially. I’m doing one clean replay from that checkpoint to confirm the saved project reproduces exactly before export.

The replay is stable (no SVD-zero parameters, maximum shift/σ 0.05) at Rwp 7.6425%. The accepted inventory remains 76.275 wt% MgWO₄, 15.618 wt% WO₃, and 8.107 wt% MgO; I’m now exporting directly from that exact checkpoint.

One edge detail surfaced: GSAS-II’s nominal 10–100° limits excluded 17 raw edge bins. Since the requested deliverable should be full-grid, I’m extending the final refinement to every measured point (9.903–100.095°) and will export all 8,301 rows.

The requested files are exported from the exact full-grid project. I’m validating CIF round-trips, phase stoichiometries, bond geometry, fraction normalization, and that `fit.csv` is numerically identical to the profile stored in `final.gpx` before writing the final report.

Completed and validated the full-grid GSAS-II refinement.

Crystalline phase fractions:

- MgWO₄: 76.253 ± 0.172 wt%
- WO₃: 15.598 ± 0.090 wt%
- MgO: 8.150 ± 0.183 wt%

Rwp = 7.616%, Rp = 5.291%, GOF = 2.666. No coherent fourth-phase reflection family was found. Fractions exclude any undetermined amorphous content; WO₃ oxygen coordinates were not independently reliable and retain the reference model.

